 <aside class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-7">
 <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">Quick Answer</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:text-xl speakable-answer">In Nevada, the employer pays for most required PPE under OSHA rule 29 CFR 1910.132(h) and must furnish any uniform distinctive in style, color, or material at no cost under NRS 608.165. Bighorn Threads supplies the branded uniforms, hi-vis, and FR garments employers buy and issue to their crews.</p>
 </aside>

 <aside class="mb-10 border-l-4 border-gold-500 bg-paper p-6">
 <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">Key Takeaways</p>
 <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li>Required PPE is the employer's cost, with a short list of exceptions.</li>
 <li>A logo shirt in company colors is likely a "distinctive" uniform under Nevada law.</li>
 <li>Ordinary clothing and weather gear are excluded from the OSHA payment rule.</li>
 <li>Replacement PPE is on the employer unless the worker lost or intentionally damaged it.</li>
 </ul>
 </aside>

 <h2 id="two-rules" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Which Laws Decide Who Pays for Workwear in Nevada?</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Two separate rules apply. Federal OSHA's PPE payment rule, enforced in Nevada by <a href="https://dir.nv.gov/OSHA/Home/" rel="noopener" class={link}>Nevada OSHA</a>, covers protective equipment required by safety standards. Nevada's wage statute, <a href="https://www.leg.state.nv.us/NRS/NRS-608.html#NRS608Sec165" rel="noopener" class={link}>NRS 608.165</a>, covers uniforms. A single garment, such as a branded hi-vis shirt, can fall under both.</p>

 <div class="mb-8 overflow-x-auto border border-hairline">
 <table class="w-full text-base text-charcoal">
 <thead class="bg-panel text-left text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-gold-600">
 <tr><th class="px-5 py-3">Item</th><th class="px-5 py-3">Rule that applies</th><th class="px-5 py-3">Who pays</th></tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline">
 <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 font-bold">Hard hat, hi-vis vest, FR or arc-rated clothing required as PPE</td><td class="px-5 py-3">29 CFR 1910.132(h) / 1926.95(d)</td><td class="px-5 py-3">Employer</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 font-bold">Logo polo or work shirt in company colors</td><td class="px-5 py-3">NRS 608.165</td><td class="px-5 py-3">Employer (distinctive uniform)</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 font-bold">Plain jeans or long-sleeve shirt the worker can wear anywhere</td><td class="px-5 py-3">1910.132(h)(4)(ii) exclusion</td><td class="px-5 py-3">Not required of employer</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 font-bold">Non-specialty steel-toe boots worn off the job</td><td class="px-5 py-3">1910.132(h)(2) exception</td><td class="px-5 py-3">Not required of employer</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 font-bold">Winter coat, rain gear, ordinary sunglasses, sunscreen</td><td class="px-5 py-3">1910.132(h)(4)(iii) exclusion</td><td class="px-5 py-3">Not required of employer</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 font-bold">Replacement for worn or damaged PPE</td><td class="px-5 py-3">1910.132(h)(5)</td><td class="px-5 py-3">Employer, unless lost or intentionally damaged</td></tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>

 <h2 id="osha-ppe" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What Does OSHA's PPE Payment Rule Say?</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal"><a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-XVII/part-1910/subpart-I/section-1910.132" rel="noopener" class={link}>29 CFR 1910.132(h)(1)</a> says protective equipment, including PPE, used to comply with OSHA standards must be provided by the employer at no cost to employees. The rule took effect in 2008. Construction employers follow the matching requirement at <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-XVII/part-1926/subpart-E/section-1926.95" rel="noopener" class={link}>29 CFR 1926.95(d)</a>.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The employer also may not require a worker to provide or pay for their own PPE unless the item is one of the listed exceptions. A worker can choose to use adequate PPE they already own, and then the employer does not have to reimburse it. When another OSHA standard states its own payment terms for specific equipment, that standard controls.</p>

 <h2 id="exceptions" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What PPE Is the Employer Not Required to Pay For?</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">OSHA lists the exceptions. Employers do not have to pay for non-specialty safety-toe footwear or non-specialty prescription safety eyewear when workers may wear them off the job. Everyday clothing such as long-sleeve shirts, long pants, street shoes, and normal work boots is excluded, as are items used only for weather protection, such as winter coats, rain gear, hats, ordinary sunglasses, and sunscreen.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The weather exclusion has limits. A plain winter jacket is excluded, but a hi-vis jacket required because a worker is exposed to traffic is PPE, because it protects against a workplace hazard rather than the weather. Read the hazard first, then the garment.</p>

 <h2 id="nevada-uniforms" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What Does Nevada's Uniform Law Require?</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">NRS 608.165 states that all uniforms or accessories distinctive as to style, color, or material shall be furnished without cost to employees by their employer. If a uniform or accessory needs a special cleaning process and cannot be easily laundered by the employee, the employer must clean it at no cost. The Nevada Labor Commissioner includes the same rule in its <a href="https://labor.nv.gov/uploadedFiles/labornvgov/content/Employer/Rules%20to%20be%20Observed%20by%20Employers%2001.16.2025.pdf" rel="noopener" class={link}>Rules to be Observed by Employers</a> poster.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Nevada's administrative code has a section interpreting "uniform" (NAC 608.090). Before charging employees for any workwear or setting a deposit policy, read the current statute and regulation or ask the <a href="https://labor.nv.gov/" rel="noopener" class={link}>Labor Commissioner's office</a>. This article is educational and is not legal advice.</p>

 <h2 id="branded" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Is a Branded Company Shirt a Distinctive Uniform?</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">A shirt with an embroidered company logo in assigned colors is hard to call ordinary clothing. Workers cannot easily wear it elsewhere, and it identifies the employer. Most Las Vegas contractors and hospitality employers treat branded tops, caps, and jackets as company-paid and plan the budget that way. When in doubt, the cautious path is to furnish them at no cost.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Laundering is the second cost to plan for. Branded cotton polos and work shirts are usually washable at home. FR garments with strict care instructions may count as needing a special cleaning process. Bighorn does not launder, repair, or inspect garments, so employers that need FR laundry use a separate service.</p>

 <h2 id="budget" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">How Should an Employer Budget a Compliant Uniform Program?</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Start with a role list. For each role, separate required PPE (hi-vis class, FR rating, hard hat), branded uniform pieces, and optional extras such as hoodies. Set issue quantities per new hire and a replacement rule for worn items. Our guide to <a href="/blog/how-many-shirts-per-crew-member-vegas/" class={link}>how many shirts per crew member</a> covers issue counts.</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Buy instead of rent.</strong> Owning garments avoids rental contracts and lets the company control replacements. See our <a href="/cintas-unifirst-aramark-alternatives-las-vegas/" class={link}>Cintas and UniFirst alternatives</a> page.</li>
 <li><strong>Issue by role.</strong> A <a href="/company-uniforms/" class={link}>company uniform program</a> or online company store keeps orders tied to approved items.</li>
 <li><strong>Document it.</strong> Record what was issued and when. That helps with replacement under 1910.132(h)(5).</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Bighorn Threads sources garments through production partners and ships branded workwear nationwide. Pricing, minimums, and timing depend on the order and are listed on the written quote.</p>

